==================================================
Lab 5: Server-Side Template Injection with Information Leak
==================================================

=======================
WHAT THIS LAB IS ABOUT
=======================

A shopping website lets a content manager edit the template that generates each product description.
The site uses the Django template engine, and the template can read the framework's settings.
One of those settings is the SECRET_KEY, which the lab asks us to find and submit.

Goal: find the Django SECRET_KEY and submit it with the "Submit solution" button.

==============================
HOW THE WEBSITE NORMALLY WORKS
==============================

A content manager logs in, opens the template editor for a product, and saves new template text.
The product page renders that template, filling in values such as product name and stock.

=======================
 WHAT IS THE PROBLEM
=======================

The template text is evaluated by Django, not displayed as plain text.
Anyone who can edit the template can reference objects the framework exposes, including the settings object.

===========================
THREE THINGS TO UNDERSTAND
===========================

Fuzz string: Invalid template syntax, such as ${{<%[%'"}}%\`, produces an error that reveals the template engine. Here, the error pointed to Django.
{% debug %}: A built-in Django tag that lists the objects and variables available to the template, including settings.
SECRET_KEY: Django's signing key. If an attacker learns it, they can forge session cookies and signed data.

======================================
THE ATTACK
======================================

First, we use {% debug %} to list what the template can access. The output includes the settings object.

Then we save this as the template:

{{settings.SECRET_KEY}}

Django renders the value of SECRET_KEY into the product page. In the lab, the key appeared in the template preview as a 32-character string.

============================
HOW TO SOLVE IT MANUALLY
============================

Log in as content-manager and open a product's template editor.
Replace the template with {% debug %} and save. Check the output for settings.
Replace the template with {{settings.SECRET_KEY}} and click Preview or Save.
Copy the rendered key exactly and paste it into the "Submit solution" button.

=========================
WHAT THIS SCRIPT DOES
=========================

* It logs in as content-manager and checks that the login worked.
* It loads the product template editor and reads the CSRF token from that page.
* It saves {{settings.SECRET_KEY}} as the template, with the fields the browser sends: csrf, template, and template-action=save.
* It loads the product page and prints the status. The key is read in the browser, since it can't be reliably matched by a pattern.

=============================
WHY THIS HAPPENS
=============================

The application lets users write templates that the framework evaluates. The template can read any setting the engine exposes,
and the secret key is one of them.

=============================
HOW TO PREVENT THIS
=============================

Do not let users edit template source. Let them change only the data that a fixed template displays.
Never expose settings objects to templates that users can influence. Keep SECRET_KEY out of template context.
Restrict the template engine to safe tags and filters. Remove debug output from production.

=============================
 KEY POINTS TO REMEMBER
=============================
Template injection can expose framework secrets, not only run commands.
Read a secret from the rendered output rather than guessing its format, since key lengths and characters vary.
Never let users control template source.
